"Be careful, though. Not every operation lets you swap. Addition does: 3 + 4 and 4 + 3 are both 7. Subtraction and division do not: 7 − 3 is 4, but 3 − 7 is not, and 12 ÷ 4 is 3, but 4 ÷ 12 is not. Order matters in daily life, too. Putting on your socks and then your shoes is not the same as shoes and then socks! One more thing: classrooms in different countries write the same tray in different orders. In the end both orders give twelve, and now you know why.": "bbe23b9bd63f",
"Count down one column at a time.": "e3b00d84843a",
"Counting equal groups like this is what multiplication is for. We write 3 × 4 and read it “three times four.” The numbers we multiply are called factors, and the answer is called the product. Things set out in rows and columns, like the cubes on the tray, make an array. Because an array can be counted by its rows or by its columns, 3 × 4 and 4 × 3 have the same product. Changing the order of the factors does not change the product. Mathematicians call this the commutative property of multiplication.": "01b2a6478625",
"In 3 × 4, the factors are 3 and 4.": "9df0f1ae39b0",
"It is not luck, and you can see why without doing any arithmetic. Each of you counted every cube on the tray exactly once. Nobody added a cube, and nobody took one away. The cubes did not care which way you were facing. In fact, you can skip the second chair. Just turn the tray a quarter of the way around. Three rows of four become four rows of three, and the pile is still the same pile.": "77f6064ac3ba",
"Multiplication is commutative, so 3 × 4 equals 4 × 3.": "1aeb5dc76133",
"Name one operation where the order does matter, and give an example.": "13002cff802d",
"Put twelve small cubes on a tray in neat rows: three rows, with four cubes in each row. Now count them. You could count one row at a time: four, eight, twelve. Your friend is sitting at the side of the table, and from there the tray looks different. Your friend sees four rows, with three cubes in each, and counts three, six, nine, twelve. You counted three fours. Your friend counted four threes. You both got twelve. Is that just luck?": "3741747ecae4",
"Put twelve small cubes on a tray in neat rows: three rows, with four cubes in each row. Now count them. You could count one row at a time: four, eight, twelve. Your friend is sitting at the side of the table, and from there the tray looks different. Your friend sees four rows, with three cubes in each, and counts three, six, nine, twelve. You counted three fours. Your friend counted four threes. You both got twelve. Is that just luck? It is not luck, and you can see why without doing any arithmetic. Each of you counted every cube on the tray exactly once. Nobody added a cube, and nobody took one away. The cubes did not care which way you were facing. In fact, you can skip the second chair. Just turn the tray a quarter of the way around. Three rows of four become four rows of three, and the pile is still the same pile. Counting equal groups like this is what multiplication is for. We write 3 × 4 and read it “three times four.” The numbers we multiply are called factors, and the answer is called the product. Things set out in rows and columns, like the cubes on the tray, make an array. Because an array can be counted by its rows or by its columns, 3 × 4 and 4 × 3 have the same product. Changing the order of the factors does not change the product. Mathematicians call this the commutative property of multiplication. This one idea saves a great deal of work. A multiplication table from 1 × 1 to 10 × 10 has one hundred squares. But every square off the diagonal has a twin: 7 × 8 and 8 × 7 are both 56. Fold the table along the diagonal, and each square lands on its twin. That leaves only fifty-five different facts to learn instead of one hundred. Learn one, and you know two. The same picture explains area. Push the cubes together until they touch, and they make a rectangle four units long and three units wide. Its area is twelve square units, because twelve unit squares cover it. Turn the rectangle, and it is three units long and four units wide. It is the same rectangle, so it has the same area. Finding the area of a rectangle and counting an array are the same job. Be careful, though. Not every operation lets you swap. Addition does: 3 + 4 and 4 + 3 are both 7. Subtraction and division do not: 7 − 3 is 4, but 3 − 7 is not, and 12 ÷ 4 is 3, but 4 ÷ 12 is not. Order matters in daily life, too. Putting on your socks and then your shoes is not the same as shoes and then socks! One more thing: classrooms in different countries write the same tray in different orders. In the end both orders give twelve, and now you know why.": "11e889ecb46b",
